# Resolution
Fixed in commit [`3955421907610177ff0bd2547322b89a1b1dd9b7`](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/3955421907610177ff0bd2547322b89a1b1dd9b7) (`Fix idle reclaim rescan scheduling`).

`wait_for_runtime_map_reclaim_work()` now performs one bounded process-local
queue poll and returns `None` when that poll expires. Control therefore returns
to the reclaim worker's outer scheduler, which re-evaluates the durable scan
deadline and rediscovers capacity-deferred or newly eligible roots without
requiring unrelated queue activity. Durable discovery remains owned by the
single managed-worker cadence; the public queue wait does not introduce a
second hidden scan owner.

The regression creates three deleted-object reclaim roots in one PG, filling
the two-entry per-PG queue and leaving the third root capacity-deferred. A
storage-instance-scoped test hook holds the next durable scan beyond the test
window, waits until the worker has drained the first two roots and returned
from an empty queue poll, and verifies that only the third root remains. It
then releases the worker and forces the next outer-loop scan, proving the
deferred root is rediscovered and reclaimed. This removes dependence on the
normal 250 ms scan interval or on how long the first two reclaims take under
load.

The deterministic gate also has an RAII release guard declared after the
worker. If the test times out or otherwise unwinds, the gate is released before
the worker is dropped and joined, preventing the test process deadlock
identified during review.

Validation for the fixing commit:

- `cargo nextest run -p server-core`: 1,203 tests passed.
- `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`: passed.
- `cargo fmt --all` and `git diff --check`: passed.

# Summary
Introduced: the new wait_for_queued_reclaim_work_poll no longer performs durable discovery, and the caller's explicit scan cadence is bypassed while the worker is blocked inside wait_for_runtime_map_reclaim_work.
The reclaim worker still schedules durable scans with next_durable_scan_at, but when no pending/deferred/in-memory queue work exists it calls wait_for_runtime_map_reclaim_work. That helper loops internally over wait_for_queued_reclaim_work_poll until a queued item appears or shutdown occurs. The poll has a timeout, but the timeout is consumed inside wait_for_runtime_map_reclaim_work, so control never returns to the outer worker loop to re-evaluate next_durable_scan_at. This is a regression from the previous wait path, which performed durable discovery on every poll. Durable reclaim roots that are intentionally not queued immediately, such as object payload reclaims deferred by per-PG capacity, lease-gated reclaims, or bucket-delete roots that become eligible after time passes, can therefore remain unreclaimed indefinitely unless unrelated new reclaim work wakes the queue. An authenticated user can create/delete enough objects in the same PG to hit the small per-PG reclaim queue capacity and leave deleted payloads on disk, causing persistent storage consumption and potential service-wide denial of service.

