## Report
Validated the finding against commit 62c20a2c242e841d6d8b1f6ae232a3a902b329ca. Code review showed durable reclaim scans are only initiated by the outer worker loop: crates/server-core/src/coordinator/runtime.rs:563-578 updates next_durable_scan_at after enqueue_durable_reclaim_work_excluding. When no immediate/deferred work exists, the worker calls wait_for_runtime_map_reclaim_work at runtime.rs:777-779. That helper loops internally at runtime.rs:1019-1030 and repeatedly calls wait_for_queued_reclaim_work_poll at line 1025. The storage-side queue wait explicitly does not perform durable discovery: crates/storage/src/cluster/request_ops.rs:10080-10090. The queue can capacity-defer object payload roots per PG at crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs:1092-1099, leaving durable roots for later scans. I added a targeted in-crate PoC test that creates 3 deleted objects in one PG with background sweepers disabled. The first two roots fill the per-PG reclaim queue, and the third remains durable/capacity-deferred. After starting the real reclaim worker, it drains the first two queued roots, then idles. The PoC waits 900ms, well beyond the 250ms durable scan interval, and confirms the third root still exists. Then a manual durable scan queues the same stranded root and the worker reclaims it, proving the root was eligible but the idle worker did not rescan. Command output: `PoC: third capacity-deferred durable reclaim root still exists after 900ms idle wait: true` and `PoC: manual durable scan queued and worker reclaimed the stranded root`. LLDB confirmed the worker was in the suspected wait path: breakpoint at runtime.rs:1025 showed thread `argmin-reclaim` in `wait_for_runtime_map_reclaim_work`, called from `ReclaimSweeper::spawn` at runtime.rs:778. Crash/ASan/valgrind were considered: this is a logic/availability bug rather than a memory-corruption crash; valgrind is not installed, and Rust ASan via `-Z sanitizer=address` is unavailable on the stable toolchain.

